   Actually, if I'm reading the code correctly, ring-wearing would work 
just the same as any other kind of magic armor that monsters can currently 
wear --- shields of reflection, speed boots, etc. The infrastructure is 
actually already there; it's just that monsters never try to put on rings.
   The tricky part is figuring out when a monster is allowed to put on a 
ring --- can it put on two rings? What if its weapon is cursed? and so on.
It turns out that the code already hacks up a solution for putting on 
armor under a worn cloak, and arbitrarily disallows putting on a shirt 
under worn armor, so the DevTeam isn't trying to make monsters strictly 
follow the rules that players have to follow w.r.t. armor.

> Well, amulet wearing was implemented and monsters only wear very few of 
> those.

   Because the put-on-an-amulet code is written to only allow two kinds of 
amulets. :)

> I can imagine that if monster rings were implemented there would be a 
> fairly small set of rings the monsters would wear:
>
> [foo] Resistance
> Conflict

   Personally, I'm not a fan of the =oConflict idea that started this 
thread. Stormbringer-like effects are too much like gratuitous instadeath 
in some situations, and I think that's the /best/ of the options suggested 
so far. Face it: conflict does things to monsters that can't be simulated 
on the player character.

> Invisibility
> Polymorph

   Eh?  I know they can jump onto poly traps, but =oPoly take a long time 
to kick in, don't they?  This wouldn't be on my short list.

> Regeneration
> Teleportation

   See =oPoly.

Rings I'd add that you didn't: invisibility, see invisible, protection, 
